CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command (Command Injection)

Description:
Command injection occurs when user-supplied input is passed to a system command execution function without proper sanitization. An attacker can inject additional commands by using shell metacharacters like ;, |, &&, ||, backticks, or $().

In C/C++, this commonly happens when user input is passed to system(), popen(), exec() family, or similar functions.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Building command strings with sprintf() using user input and passing to system()
- Using popen() with unsanitized input
- Concatenating user input into shell commands

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    char command[256];
    sprintf(command, "ping -c 1 %s", user_input);
    system(command);
    // If user_input is: "localhost; rm -rf /"
    // Executes: ping -c 1 localhost; rm -rf /

    char cmd[512];
    sprintf(cmd, "cat /var/log/%s", filename);
    FILE* fp = popen(cmd, "r");
    // If filename is: "test; cat /etc/passwd"
    // Reads password file

Fix:
    // Validate input against whitelist
    for (int i = 0; user_input[i]; i++) {
        if (!isalnum(user_input[i]) && user_input[i] != '.') {
            fprintf(stderr, "Invalid input\n");
            return -1;
        }
    }
    // Use execvp() instead of system() to avoid shell interpretation
    char* args[] = {"ping", "-c", "1", user_input, NULL};
    execvp("ping", args);

Severity: CRITICAL
Impact: Full system compromise, arbitrary command execution, data breach
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html

Related CWEs: CWE-77 (Command Injection), CWE-88 (Improper Neutralization of Argument Delimiters)

CWE-825: Expired Pointer Dereference (Dangling Pointer)

Description:
A dangling pointer is a pointer that references memory that has been freed or is no longer valid. Dereferencing a dangling pointer leads to undefined behavior including crashes, data corruption, or code execution. This commonly occurs with returning stack-local variables, realloc that moves memory, and pointer aliasing after free.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Returning a pointer to a local (stack) variable from a function
- Using an alias pointer after realloc moves the original memory block
- Storing pointers to objects that go out of scope
- Iterator invalidation in C++ containers after modification

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    // Returning pointer to local variable
    char* get_greeting(const char* name) {
        char buffer[64];
        sprintf(buffer, "Hello, %s!", name);
        return buffer;  // buffer is on the stack, invalid after return
    }

    // Dangling pointer via realloc
    int* arr = (int*)malloc(5 * sizeof(int));
    int* alias = arr;
    arr = (int*)realloc(arr, 50 * sizeof(int));  // may move memory
    alias[0] = 42;  // dangling if realloc moved the block

Fix:
    // Use heap allocation instead of returning stack pointer
    char* get_greeting(const char* name) {
        char* buffer = (char*)malloc(256);
        if (buffer == NULL) return NULL;
        snprintf(buffer, 256, "Hello, %s!", name);
        return buffer;  // Caller must free
    }

    // Update all aliases after realloc
    int* arr = (int*)malloc(5 * sizeof(int));
    arr = (int*)realloc(arr, 50 * sizeof(int));
    if (arr == NULL) { /* handle error */ }
    // Don't keep old aliases

Severity: HIGH
Impact: Undefined behavior, crashes, potential code execution
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/825.html

Related CWEs: CWE-416 (Use After Free), CWE-562 (Return of Stack Variable Address)

CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound

Description:
An integer overflow occurs when an arithmetic operation attempts to create a numeric value that is outside the range that can be represented by the data type. In C/C++, signed integer overflow is undefined behavior, while unsigned integers wrap around. This can lead to incorrect calculations, buffer overflows, and security vulnerabilities.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Adding to INT_MAX (2147483647 for 32-bit signed int) causing wraparound to negative
- Multiplying large integers without overflow checking
- Using integer results for memory allocation sizes (can allocate tiny buffer for large data)
- Signed/unsigned comparison issues where negative values compare as large positive

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    int a = 2147483647;  // INT_MAX
    int b = a + 1;       // Undefined behavior: signed overflow
    printf("Result: %d\n", b);  // Likely prints -2147483648

    // Dangerous for allocation:
    int count = user_input;  // Could be very large
    int size = count * sizeof(int);  // Could overflow to small value
    int* arr = malloc(size);  // Allocates tiny buffer
    // Writing count elements overflows the buffer

    // Signed/unsigned comparison:
    unsigned int len = 10;
    int index = -1;
    if (index < len) { ... }  // -1 becomes large unsigned, comparison fails

Fix:
    // Check before arithmetic
    if (a > INT_MAX - 1) {
        // Handle overflow
    }
    int b = a + 1;

    // Safe allocation
    if (count > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(int)) {
        // Handle overflow
    }
    size_t size = (size_t)count * sizeof(int);

    // Use same signedness for comparison
    if (index >= 0 && (unsigned int)index < len) { ... }

Severity: MEDIUM to HIGH
Impact: Buffer overflow, incorrect calculations, denial of service, potential code execution
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html

Related CWEs: CWE-191 (Integer Underflow), CWE-681 (Incorrect Conversion between Numeric Types), CWE-195 (Signed to Unsigned Conversion Error)

CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime (Memory Leak)

Description:
Memory is allocated dynamically (via malloc, calloc, realloc, or new) but is never freed, causing the program to consume increasing amounts of memory over time. In long-running programs like servers or daemons, memory leaks can eventually exhaust available memory and cause the program or system to crash.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Allocating memory with malloc() but never calling free()
- Returning from a function early without freeing allocated memory
- Overwriting a pointer to allocated memory without freeing it first
- Exception/error paths that skip cleanup code
- Allocating in a loop without freeing each iteration

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    int* leak = (int*)malloc(100 * sizeof(int));
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
        leak[i] = i;
    }
    // Function returns without free(leak) -> memory leak

    // Lost pointer:
    char* buf = malloc(1024);
    buf = malloc(2048);  // Original 1024-byte block is leaked

    // Early return leak:
    void process() {
        char* data = malloc(512);
        if (error_condition) return;  // data is leaked
        free(data);
    }

Fix:
    int* data = (int*)malloc(100 * sizeof(int));
    if (data == NULL) return -1;
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
        data[i] = i;
    }
    // ... use data ...
    free(data);  // Always free when done

    // Use goto for cleanup in C:
    void process() {
        char* data = malloc(512);
        if (error_condition) goto cleanup;
        // ... use data ...
    cleanup:
        free(data);
    }

Severity: MEDIUM
Impact: Denial of service (memory exhaustion), degraded performance, system instability
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html

Related CWEs: CWE-772 (Missing Release of Resource after Effective Lifetime), CWE-404 (Improper Resource Shutdown or Release)

CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference

Description:
A NULL pointer dereference occurs when the application dereferences a pointer that it expects to be valid but is NULL. This typically causes a segmentation fault or crash. In C/C++, this is a common bug when return values from functions like malloc(), fopen(), or other allocation/lookup functions are not checked before use.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Not checking if malloc() returned NULL before using the pointer
- Not checking if fopen() returned NULL before reading/writing the file
- Conditionally assigning a pointer but unconditionally using it later
- Assuming function parameters are never NULL without validation

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    FILE* fp = fopen("config.txt", "r");
    fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp);  // fp could be NULL if file doesn't exist

    char* ptr = NULL;
    if (argc > 5)
        ptr = "valid";
    printf("Ptr: %s\n", ptr);  // ptr is NULL when argc <= 5

    int* data = (int*)malloc(large_size);
    data[0] = 42;  // data could be NULL if allocation failed

Fix:
    FILE* fp = fopen("config.txt", "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open config.txt\n");
        return 1;
    }
    fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp);
    fclose(fp);

    char* ptr = NULL;
    if (argc > 5)
        ptr = "valid";
    if (ptr != NULL)
        printf("Ptr: %s\n", ptr);

Severity: MEDIUM to HIGH
Impact: Denial of service (crash), potential code execution in some environments
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html

Related CWEs: CWE-690 (Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference), CWE-252 (Unchecked Return Value)

CWE-193: Off-by-one Error

Description:
An off-by-one error occurs when a loop iterates one time too many or too few, or when an array index calculation is incorrect by one. This is one of the most common programming errors in C/C++ and frequently leads to buffer overflows, reading uninitialized memory, or skipping elements.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Using <= instead of < in a loop bound (writing one past the end of an array)
- Allocating n bytes but writing n+1 bytes (forgetting null terminator)
- Starting a loop at 1 instead of 0 (skipping first element)
- Fence-post errors in calculating array sizes

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    // Off-by-one: writes to scores[10] which is out of bounds
    int* scores = (int*)malloc(10 * sizeof(int));
    for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i++) {  // Should be i < 10
        scores[i] = i * 10;  // scores[10] is one past the allocated memory
    }

    // Missing null terminator space
    char* contents = (char*)malloc(size);     // Should be malloc(size + 1)
    fread(contents, 1, size, fp);
    // contents is not null-terminated -> string functions will overrun

    // String copy off-by-one
    char buf[10];
    for (int i = 0; i <= strlen(src); i++)  // Copies null but may overflow
        buf[i] = src[i];

Fix:
    int* scores = (int*)malloc(10 * sizeof(int));
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {  // Correct: i < 10
        scores[i] = i * 10;
    }

    char* contents = (char*)malloc(size + 1);  // +1 for null terminator
    fread(contents, 1, size, fp);
    contents[size] = '\0';

Severity: MEDIUM to HIGH
Impact: Buffer overflow, data corruption, crashes, potential code execution
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/193.html

Related CWEs: CWE-120 (Buffer Copy without Checking Size), CWE-787 (Out-of-bounds Write), CWE-131 (Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size)

CWE-362: Concurrent Execution using Shared Resource with Improper Synchronization (Race Condition)

Description:
A race condition occurs when multiple threads or processes access shared data concurrently without proper synchronization, and the outcome depends on the timing of execution. This can corrupt data, cause crashes, or create security vulnerabilities. In C/C++, this commonly occurs with global variables accessed from multiple pthreads without mutexes.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Multiple threads incrementing/modifying a shared global variable without a mutex
- TOCTOU (Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use): checking a condition then acting on it non-atomically
- Accessing shared data structures (linked lists, arrays) from multiple threads without locks
- Signal handlers modifying shared state

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    // Race condition: no synchronization
    int global_counter = 0;
    void* increment(void* arg) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++)
            global_counter++;  // Not atomic, data race
        return NULL;
    }
    // Two threads running increment() simultaneously
    // Expected: 200000, Actual: unpredictable (< 200000)

    // TOCTOU vulnerability
    if (access(filename, W_OK) == 0) {   // Check
        // Window: attacker replaces file with symlink
        FILE* fp = fopen(filename, "w");  // Use: may write to different file
        fprintf(fp, "%s", data);
        fclose(fp);
    }

Fix:
    // Use mutex for thread synchronization
    pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
    void* increment(void* arg) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
            pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
            global_counter++;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        }
        return NULL;
    }

    // For TOCTOU: open with proper flags, check after opening
    int fd = open(filename, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_NOFOLLOW, 0644);
    if (fd >= 0) {
        // Use fd directly, no separate check needed
    }

Severity: MEDIUM to HIGH
Impact: Data corruption, denial of service, privilege escalation, security bypass
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/362.html

Related CWEs: CWE-367 (TOCTOU Race Condition), CWE-820 (Missing Synchronization), CWE-366 (Race Condition within a Thread)

CWE-89: SQL Injection (Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an SQL Command)

Description:
SQL injection occurs when user-supplied data is inserted into an SQL query without proper sanitization or parameterization. An attacker can manipulate the query to access, modify, or delete unauthorized data, bypass authentication, or execute administrative operations on the database.

In C/C++ programs, this typically happens when user input is concatenated into SQL query strings using sprintf(), strcat(), or string formatting before being passed to database APIs like sqlite3_exec(), mysql_query(), or ODBC functions.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Building SQL queries with sprintf() using user input
- Concatenating user input directly into query strings with strcat()
- Using string formatting to insert user parameters into queries
- Not using parameterized queries / prepared statements

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    void process_input(char* input) {
        char query[128];
        sprintf(query, "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '%s'", input);
        // If input is: ' OR '1'='1' --
        // Query becomes: SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '' OR '1'='1' --'
        // This returns ALL users, bypassing authentication
        sqlite3_exec(db, query, callback, 0, &err);
    }

Fix:
    // Use parameterized queries (prepared statements)
    sqlite3_stmt* stmt;
    sqlite3_prepare_v2(db, "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", -1, &stmt, NULL);
    sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, input, -1, SQLITE_STATIC);
    sqlite3_step(stmt);
    sqlite3_finalize(stmt);

    // Or validate/escape input (less preferred)
    // Whitelist allowed characters
    // Reject input containing ', ", --, ;, etc.

Severity: CRITICAL
Impact: Data breach, data loss, authentication bypass, full system compromise
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html

Related CWEs: CWE-20 (Improper Input Validation), CWE-77 (Command Injection), CWE-564 (SQL Injection: Hibernate)

CWE-457: Use of Uninitialized Variable

Description:
The code uses a variable that has not been initialized, leading to unpredictable behavior. In C/C++, local variables on the stack are not automatically initialized and contain whatever data was previously in that memory location. Using uninitialized variables can lead to crashes, incorrect logic, and information disclosure.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Declaring a pointer without initializing it, then dereferencing it
- Using a variable before assigning it a value in all code paths
- Partially initialized structs or arrays
- Relying on stack memory to be zeroed (it is not)

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    int* data;              // Uninitialized pointer
    printf("%d\n", *data);  // Dereferences garbage address -> crash or worse

    int result;
    if (condition)
        result = compute();
    printf("%d\n", result);  // Uninitialized if condition is false

    struct config cfg;
    cfg.name = "test";
    // cfg.timeout not set
    use_config(&cfg);  // Reads uninitialized timeout field

Fix:
    int* data = NULL;       // Initialize to NULL
    if (data != NULL)
        printf("%d\n", *data);

    int result = 0;  // Always initialize
    if (condition)
        result = compute();
    printf("%d\n", result);

    struct config cfg = {0};  // Zero-initialize all fields
    cfg.name = "test";
    cfg.timeout = 30;
    use_config(&cfg);

Severity: MEDIUM to HIGH
Impact: Undefined behavior, crashes, information disclosure, incorrect program logic
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/457.html

Related CWEs: CWE-908 (Use of Uninitialized Resource), CWE-824 (Access of Uninitialized Pointer)

CWE-416: Use After Free

Description:
Referencing memory after it has been freed can cause a program to crash, use unexpected values, or execute arbitrary code. When memory is freed, its contents may remain intact and accessible through a dangling pointer, but this behavior is undefined and unreliable.

Common Vulnerable Patterns:
- Calling free() on a pointer and then reading/writing through that pointer
- Double free: calling free() on the same pointer twice
- Returning a pointer to freed memory for use by a caller
- Storing a pointer to freed memory in a data structure and later accessing it

Example of Vulnerable Code:
    char* buf = (char*)malloc(256);
    strcpy(buf, "sensitive data");
    free(buf);
    printf("Freed: %s\n", buf);  // Use after free: undefined behavior

    // Double free example:
    int* scores = (int*)malloc(10 * sizeof(int));
    free(scores);
    free(scores);  // Double free: heap corruption

Fix:
    char* buf = (char*)malloc(256);
    strcpy(buf, "sensitive data");
    free(buf);
    buf = NULL;  // Set to NULL after freeing
    // Don't access buf after free

    // For double free prevention:
    free(scores);
    scores = NULL;  // Prevents double free since free(NULL) is safe

Severity: HIGH to CRITICAL
Impact: Arbitrary code execution, denial of service, information disclosure
CWE URL: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/416.html

Related CWEs: CWE-415 (Double Free), CWE-825 (Expired Pointer Dereference), CWE-672 (Operation on a Resource after Expiration or Release)
